  "body": "This is the STEP side of #370. It follows the split agreed on the issue with the #368 holder: a reader that returns a vendor tool STEP's named coordinate systems as plain data. It adds no tool-body component, command, project field or UI. Turning a found system into #368's numeric placement is the next round, which stacks on `issue/368-tool-body-step` once that branch is pushed.\n\n## What changed\n- New `crates/app/src/geometry/import/step_csys.rs`:\n  - `read_tool_csys(&[u8])` returns every named `AXIS2_PLACEMENT_3D` with its name, entity id and role, its origin in mm (scaled by the file's length unit), and its unit z and x axes.\n  - `CsysRole::classify` maps the ISO/TS 13399 names below, ignoring case. Any other name is `Other` and is never used for alignment.\n  - `find(role)` returns the single system with that role, or the named reason there isn't one (missing, refused or ambiguous).\n  - `none_found()` returns the named reason a caller falls back to numeric placement.\n  - Refusals are by name, never a panic:\n    - a zero or non-finite direction;\n    - a non-finite origin;\n    - a reference direction parallel to the axis;\n    - a dangling or wrong-kind reference;\n    - one name used for two different placements.\n- `step_units.rs`: three helpers are now visible inside the module (`pub(super)`).\n- Three original, hand-written fixtures in `testdata/models/tool_csys_*.step`.\n- `docs/decisions/370-tool-csys-align.md`\n\n## Names, confirmed before any code\nThe names come from public ISO/TS 13399 preview pages (iTeh samples of parts 303, 401 and 405) and are cited in the provenance comment and the decisions file:\n- PCS (primary coordinate system)\n- MCS (mounting coordinate system)\n- CIP (coordinate system in process)\n- CSIF (coordinate system interface)\n- CSW / CSWx / CSWx_y (coordinate system workpiece side)\n\nCRP is a point, not a coordinate system, so it is not read.\n\n## Readings\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-app --test tool_csys`: 6/6 pass. The existing `import` suite and the `step_units` unit tests still pass.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n- `scripts/gate.sh quick upstream/main`: every app suite passes except `scripting`, which SIGSEGV'd once under concurrent load.\n  - This branch touches nothing on the scripting path.\n  - The same binary passed 45 of 45 reruns alone.\n  - Filed as #379 (P1).\n\n## Pinning tests\n- `iso_13399_coordinate_systems_are_found_and_classified`\n- `a_file_without_tool_coordinate_systems_says_so_by_name`\n- `degenerate_or_ambiguous_coordinate_systems_are_refused_by_name`\n- `origins_read_in_the_files_length_unit`\n- `iso_13399_names_classify_and_others_do_not`\n- `unreadable_input_is_a_clean_error_never_a_panic`\n\n## Not exercised\n- No real vendor STEP package was tried; the fixtures are hand-written.\n- Assembly transforms are not composed: coordinates are in the frame of the part that holds them. Same-named systems in different frames give the ambiguity refusal, not a silent pick.\n- The issue's \"aligns with no manual offsets\" waits for #368's body model, so this PR is `Refs`, not `Fixes`.\n\nChangelog: none (reader only; no user-visible behaviour until the #368 wiring)\n\nRefs #370\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
